\title{Getting Started with \texttt{DistFamilyFit}}
\author{\c{S}ule \c{S}evval Karakaya Demir, Vilda Purut\c{c}uo\u{g}lu, Ahmet Bursal\i}
\date{Version 0.1.1}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
<<setup,echo=FALSE,results=hide>>=
library(DistFamilyFit)
set.seed(2026)
@

\section{Scope and input format}
This package implements the methodology of \cite{karakaya2026}, where the
families below are compared on mobile application usage variables and Johnson
transformations are used for dependence analysis.

The package fits continuous distribution families. Johnson \cite{johnson1949}
is the default; Pearson \cite{pearson1895}, Burr \cite{burr1942,tadikamalla1980}
and the generalized lambda distribution (GLD) \cite{ramberg1974,freimer1988}
are selectable; background on these systems is given in \cite{jkb1994}.
Multivariate modeling currently uses Johnson marginals only. Independent fits
to several columns must not be described as a joint distribution model.

A comma-separated values (CSV) or tab-separated values (TSV) input must be rectangular and contain a unique, nonempty header.
Each row represents one observation and each selected numeric column represents
one variable. Values in a multivariate row must refer to the same observation.
ID, date and category fields may remain in the file but must be excluded through
column selection. Set field separators and decimal marks explicitly. Do not put
units, percentage symbols or thousands separators in numeric cells.

\begin{verbatim}
sample_id,variable_1,variable_2
s001,12.5,-1.7
s002,18.3,0.4
\end{verbatim}

The tiny table illustrates format only. The default minimum sample size is 20;
it is a software requirement, not a guarantee of reliable statistical inference.
The bundled template contains 30 artificial observations:
<<read-template>>=
path <- system.file('extdata', 'input_template.csv', package = 'DistFamilyFit')
prepared <- read_fit_data(path, columns = c('variable_1', 'variable_2'))
head(prepared$data, 3)
prepared$audit$n_retained
@

For a semicolon-delimited decimal-comma file, use \texttt{sep=';'} and
\texttt{dec=','}. The reader never guesses these settings. Missing values are
blank or \texttt{NA} by default. Omission is explicit and complete-case across
selected columns; all removed original row numbers are retained in the audit.
Zeros, negative values and outliers are not silently removed.
<<prepare-memory>>=
raw <- data.frame(id = seq_len(60), value = sinh(rnorm(60)))
clean <- prepare_fit_data(raw, columns = 'value')
clean$audit$ignored_columns
@

\section{One variable and family selection}
<<univariate>>=
x <- sinh(rnorm(120))
u <- fit_univariate(x)
u$ranking[, c('model', 'KS', 'status')]
@

The ranking uses the classical Kolmogorov--Smirnov (KS) distance. The
plotting-position distance \texttt{D\_CDF}, $\max_i |F(x_{(i)}) - (i-0.5)/n|$,
is reported in a separate column. MatchRate is 100 times one minus
\texttt{D\_CDF}; it is not classification accuracy, model probability
or a goodness-of-fit p-value. All of these are descriptive in-sample measures.
<<select-family>>=
b <- fit_univariate(exp(x / 2), families = 'Burr')
g <- fit_univariate(x, families = 'GLD')
j <- fit_johnson_univariate(x, types = c('SU', 'SB'))
comparison <- fit_univariate(x, families = c('Johnson', 'GLD'))
if (requireNamespace('PearsonDS', quietly = TRUE)) {
  p <- fit_univariate(x, families = 'Pearson')
}
@

Use \texttt{families='all'} to include every implemented family. The optional
PearsonDS package supplies Pearson moment matching; the other families do not
require it. Burr III/XII candidates require strictly positive data. A failed
candidate is recorded rather than deleting observations for that candidate.

Johnson uses profiled likelihood and bounded numerical searches on standardized
scales. No normal or lognormal fallback is performed. Reaching a numerical search boundary triggers a warning and suppresses
AIC for that candidate. Johnson SL uses three identifiable parameters. GLD uses
FKML quantile matching \cite{chalabi2012}; Pearson uses empirical moments with divisor n consistently. AIC is not reported for these
non-likelihood estimators or for subsample estimation.

\section{Distribution evaluation and separate plots}
<<evaluate>>=
cdf_fit(u, c(-1, 0, 1))
quantile_fit(u, c(.1, .5, .9))
density_fit(u, c(-1, 0, 1))
transform_johnson(u, c(-1, 0, 1))
@

Parameters are internally standardized, but evaluation functions use the original
units. Fitting functions do not create plots. Call a plotting function explicitly:
<<cdf-plot,fig=TRUE,width=6,height=4>>=
(function() {
  oldpar <- par(no.readonly = TRUE)
  on.exit({
    par(oldpar)
    par(mfg = oldpar$mfg, new = oldpar$new)
  }, add = TRUE)
plot_fit(u, type = 'cdf')
})()

@

<<comparison-plot,fig=TRUE,width=6,height=4>>=
(function() {
  oldpar <- par(no.readonly = TRUE)
  on.exit({
    par(oldpar)
    par(mfg = oldpar$mfg, new = oldpar$new)
  }, add = TRUE)
plot_comparison(comparison)
})()

@

Quantile--quantile (Q--Q) and density plots are available through \texttt{plot\_fit} with the
corresponding type argument.

\section{Johnson multivariate model}
For joint fitting, rows are paired observations. Complete-case processing takes
place before marginal estimation. This differs from estimating each marginal
from a different subset and only later taking complete transformed rows.
<<multivariate>>=
z <- rnorm(120)
w <- .6 * z + sqrt(1 - .6^2) * rnorm(120)
paired <- data.frame(first = sinh(z), second = sinh(w))
m <- fit_johnson_multivariate(paired, types = 'SU')
m$marginal_diagnostics[, c('model', 'KS', 'status')]
round(m$correlation_Z, 3)
@

The covariance and correlation describe transformed Z variables, not the original
measurements. Marginal normality does not imply joint normality. The sample
covariance uses divisor n-1; the normal maximum likelihood estimation (MLE) covariance uses n. Singular covariance
is reported and regularization must be explicitly requested through ridge.
Information criteria in criteria\_Z describe only the conditional Z-normal model,
not the original-scale model including estimated marginal parameters. They must not be used to compare models with different fitted transformations.
<<joint-plot,fig=TRUE,width=6,height=4>>=
(function() {
  oldpar <- par(no.readonly = TRUE)
  on.exit({
    par(oldpar)
    par(mfg = oldpar$mfg, new = oldpar$new)
  }, add = TRUE)
plot_transformed(m, type = 'mahalanobis')
})()

@

The chi-square reference in this plot is descriptive because transformations and
parameters were estimated. It is not a calibrated hypothesis test.

\section{Diagnostics and limitations}
OK means that the implemented computation checks passed; it does not establish
good statistical fit. Warning candidates may be selected, so their notes must
be reviewed. Unstable candidates fail numerical consistency checks and are
excluded from automatic selection. Failed candidates retain their failure reason.
The smallest KS among usable candidates does not prove model acceptance.

Count data and repeated point masses are only continuous approximations in this
version. Explicit positive-component fitting describes a conditional distribution,
not a zero-inflated model. Natural support restrictions such as [0,1] are not
imposed on every family automatically. Time series and repeated measurements
need additional consideration of dependence between rows. Bootstrap-calibrated
acceptance tests and general multivariate Pearson, Burr or GLD models are not
implemented.

\section{Estimation and extreme tails}
Pearson uses moment matching, while FKML GLD uses quantile matching and adaptive
bisection to invert its quantile function for CDF evaluation. Johnson and Burr
use numerical maximum likelihood. Log-space Burr calculations stabilize the
same model. Finite precision limits extreme-tail evaluation; routine round-trip
diagnostics principally cover the 1\%--99\% quantile range and do not certify
more extreme tails. A search-boundary warning does not indicate substitution
by another distribution.

\section{Large samples and random numbers}
The argument \texttt{max\_fit\_n} estimates parameters on a random subsample
when the data are larger than this size. By default (\texttt{seed = NULL}) the
subsample uses the current random number stream, so \texttt{set.seed()} called
beforehand makes it reproducible. Supplying \texttt{seed} applies a seed
locally and restores the previous random number state afterwards.
<<subsample>>=
big <- sinh(rnorm(2000))
s1 <- fit_univariate(big, johnson_types = 'SU', max_fit_n = 300, seed = 1)
s1$audit$n_estimation
@

