\section{Detailed terminology and interpretation}
\begin{description}
\item[CDF and ECDF.] A cumulative distribution function (CDF) is the model
probability $P(X\leq x)$. An empirical cumulative distribution function (ECDF)
is the fraction of observations at or below $x$. A density is different from
an exact-value probability for continuous data.
\item[KS.] The Kolmogorov--Smirnov (KS) distance is the largest fitted-versus-
empirical cumulative probability difference, including both sides of sample
steps. It is a descriptive distance here; the package does not supply a
standard fitted-parameter significance-test p-value.
\item[MLE and NLL.] Maximum likelihood estimation (MLE) maximizes model
likelihood; negative log-likelihood (NLL) is minus the sum of log densities.
The Johnson and Burr candidates use numerical likelihood estimation. Numerical
convergence does not prove a global optimum.
\item[AIC and BIC.] The Akaike information criterion (AIC) is
$2k-2\ell$; the Bayesian information criterion (BIC) is $k\log(n)-2\ell$,
where $k$ is the compatible model's parameter count, $n$ is the number of
observations and $\ell$ is its log-likelihood. Boundary, subsampling,
non-likelihood and regularization restrictions are documented in the function
help. Joint criteria describe only a normal model on a fixed transformed
$Z$ data set; they exclude marginal estimation and cannot compare different
fitted transformations.
\item[RMSE.] Root mean square error (RMSE) is the square root of the average
squared discrepancy. The \texttt{CDF\_quantile\_RMSE} field compares fitted
cumulative probabilities at empirical quantiles with the probabilities
0.1, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 0.9, 0.95 and 0.99. It is on the probability scale,
not in original measurement units.
\item[SU, SL and SB.] These Johnson labels mean unbounded, lognormal and
bounded forms. The unbounded form extends over the real line, the lognormal
form has a finite lower endpoint, and the bounded form has two finite endpoints.
They describe support and transformation families, not levels of fit quality.
\item[GLD and FKML.] The generalized lambda distribution (GLD) uses the
Freimer--Kollia--Mudholkar--Lin (FKML) parameterization. It is fitted by
quantile matching, not by maximum likelihood. The cumulative distribution
function is evaluated by numerical inversion of its quantile function.
\item[L-BFGS-B.] This is the limited-memory Broyden--Fletcher--Goldfarb--Shanno
algorithm with box constraints. It numerically optimizes profiled Johnson
parameters within finite search guards; those guards are not true support endpoints.
\item[Q--Q.] A quantile--quantile (Q--Q) plot compares model and observed
quantiles. Extreme-point deviations can remain when central agreement is good.
The squared Mahalanobis reference plot is also descriptive.
\item[RNG.] A random number generator (RNG) produces pseudorandom draws.
With the default \texttt{seed = NULL}, subsampling uses and advances the
current random number generator state. An explicit seed is applied locally and
the previous state is restored. Reproducibility does not eliminate
sampling uncertainty.
\end{description}

The installed glossary, available as \texttt{help("DistFamilyFit\_glossary")}, also
explains file formats, encoding, R-specific identifiers and remaining output labels.


\end{document}
